2153		and~\ref{lem:qld-4-13-established} give a strategy with parameters
2154		$(q,2m+2,d,1)$ and success probability $1-\delta_{\mathrm{combine}}$; the
2155		axis-parallel clause supplies the required degree-$d$ answer format. The
2156		numerical estimate available for $\delta_{\mathrm{combine}}$ is
2157		$C \cdot m \cdot \poly(\eps,md/q)$, not the unproved form
2158		$\poly(m^2\eps,md/q)$ printed in Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-4-13}
2159		(Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}). The error function $\delta_S$
2160		absorbs the prefactor $m$ into $(md)^a$.
2161		(For $(q, 2m+2, d, 1)$ to be a parameter tuple of
2162		Definition~\ref{def:ld-game} the dimension $2m+2$ must divide $q$;
2163		admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ supplies only that $m$ divides $q$, and for
2164		admissible parameters with $m \ge 2$ the divisibility fails, so this
2165		instantiation is an unresolved obligation of the source argument,
2166		recorded in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and analyzed in
2167		\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.) Since $\{\hat{Q}^{\ell}_{f}\}$ is
2168		only a POVM, it is first made projective by Naimark dilation
2169		(Theorem~\ref{thm:naimark}), which preserves the $\simeq$ conclusions of
2170		Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-4-13}. The quantum soundness of the classical test
2171		(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, applied with $m$ replaced by $2m+2$ and
2172		$\eps$ by $\delta_{\mathrm{combine}}$) then yields a POVM
2173		$\{\hat{S}_g\}$ with outcomes $g \in \mathrm{ideg}_{d,2m+2}(\F_q)$ that
2174		is $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}$-self-consistent and
2175		$\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}$-consistent with the points measurements
2176		$\{\hat{Q}^{x,z,\alpha,\beta}_{a}\}$, where
2177		$\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}
2178		= a(md)^a(\delta_{\mathrm{combine}}^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$
2179		for universal constants $a, b$; a second application of Naimark dilation
2180		makes $\{\hat{S}_g\}$ projective with the same guarantees. Let
2181		$\mathcal{G}$ be the set of polynomials of the form
2182		$g(x,z,\alpha,\beta) = \alpha g_X(x) + \beta g_Z(z)$ with
2183		$g_X, g_Z \in \mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\F_q)$. A chain of approximations
2184		built from the consistency of $\{\hat{S}_g\}$ with the points
2185		measurements and the relations
2186		\eqref{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat}--\eqref{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat2} of
2187		Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-4-12} (using Lemma~\ref{fact:agreement} to convert
2188		$\simeq$ into $\approx$ and Lemma~\ref{fact:add-a-proj2}) correlates
2189		$\hat{S}_g$ with the indicator-weighted products of the $X$ and $Z$
2190		point measurements in both orders. Combining this with Schwartz--Zippel
2191		case analyses (Lemma~\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}, applied to
2192		polynomials of total degree at most $(2m+2)d$) --- first ruling out the
2193		$g$ that are not linear in $(\alpha, \beta)$, then, among the linear
2194		ones $g = \alpha g_1(x,z) + \beta g_2(x,z)$, ruling out those whose
2195		coefficient polynomials $g_1$ or $g_2$ depend on the wrong block of
2196		variables --- yields
2197		$\sum_{g \notin \mathcal{G}}
2198		\| (\hat{S}_g)_{\mathsf{A}\mathsf{A}'} \ket{\hat\psi} \|^2
2199		\le O(\delta_{\mathrm{ld}} + \delta_Q + md/q)
2200		=: \delta_{\mathcal{G}}$.
