2235	\begin{remark}[Divisibility obstruction in the application of the classical
2236		soundness theorem]
2237		\label{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility}
2238		Definition~\ref{def:ld-game} defines the classical low individual
2239		degree game only when its dimension parameter divides $q$: the index
2240		function $\chi$ of the question distribution
2241		(Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}) partitions $\F_q$ into
2242		classes of equal size, one for each coordinate. The same divisibility
2243		underlies the line-point distribution over $\F_q^{2m+2}$
2244		(Definition~\ref{def:line-point-dist} instantiated at dimension
2245		$2m+2$), and with it the statements of Lemmas~\ref{lem:qld-sublines}
2246		and~\ref{lem:qld-4-13} and the proofs of all three of
2247		Lemmas~\ref{lem:qld-sublines}, \ref{lem:qld-4-13}
2248		and~\ref{lem:qld-4-7}. The condition that $2m+2$ divide $q$ does not
2249		follow from admissibility of $(q,m,d)$
2250		(Definition~\ref{def:admissible}), which supplies only that $m$
2251		divides $q$; indeed the two are jointly satisfiable only in a
2252		degenerate range. An admissible field size
2253		(Definition~\ref{def:admissible-size}) is a power of two, so $m$
2254		dividing $q$ forces $m$ to be a power of two; for $m \ge 2$ the
2255		integer $m+1$ is odd and exceeds $1$, so $2m+2 = 2(m+1)$ divides no
2256		power of two, while for $m = 1$ the condition $4 \mid q$ holds for
2257		every admissible $q$ except $q = 2$. The source proof in
2258		\cite{Ji2020MIPStar} performs the instantiation at dimension $2m+2$
2259		without addressing the divisibility. The statements above are
2260		therefore retained in their source form, and for admissible
2261		parameters with $m \ge 2$ their proofs, like the source's, are
2262		blocked at the marked steps, so the conclusions are not established
2263		for such parameters, here or in the source. A proof covering every
2264		admissible parameter tuple requires a formulation of the game, of its
2265		question distribution, and of Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness} that
2266		applies to dimensions not dividing $q$; the missing step and the
2267		candidate repairs are analyzed in
2268		\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
2269	\end{remark}
